---
import Journey from '../../layouts/Journey.astro';
import Step from '../../components/journey/Step.astro';
import More from '../../components/journey/More.astro';
import Next from '../../components/journey/Next.astro';
import Fact from '../../components/data/Fact.astro';
import Verified from '../../components/data/Verified.astro';
import ForLevel from '../../components/level/ForLevel.astro';
import Term from '../../components/journey/Term.astro';
import LessonRef from '../../components/journey/LessonRef.astro';
import { models, lineup } from '../../data/models';
import { plans } from '../../data/plans';
import { sums, usd2, usd3, JOBS_PER_HOUR, JOB_TURNS } from '../../journey/chapters/meter';
import { url } from '../../lib/nav';

// The three models the reader can pick, with their list prices per million tokens. The job and its
// token counts are made up in the script; every price comes from here.
const picks = ['claude-opus-5-5', 'claude-fable-5-2', 'gpt-6-2-sol'] as const;
const short = (name: string) => name.replace(/^Claude /, '');
const priced = Object.fromEntries(picks.map((id) => {
  const p = models[id].price as { input: { value: number }; output: { value: number }; cacheRead?: { value: number } };
  return [id, { name: short(models[id].name), input: p.input.value, output: p.output.value, cacheRead: p.cacheRead?.value }];
}));
const data = { models: priced, cacheWrite: lineup.cacheWrite5m.value, proPrice: plans.claude.pro.price.value };
const priceIds = picks.flatMap((id) => ['input', 'output', ...('cacheRead' in models[id].price ? ['cacheRead'] : [])].map((f) => `models.${id}.price.${f}`));
const ids = [
  ...priceIds, 'lineup.cacheWrite5m', 'lineup.opusCacheWrite5m', 'lineup.cacheReadOpusSonnet', 'lineup.cacheBreakers', 'lineup.claudeCodeCacheTtl', 'plans.claude.pro.price',
  'plans.claude.limits.wholeConversation', 'plans.claude.limits.whatUsageMeans', 'plans.claude.limits.fiveHour', 'plans.claude.limits.weekly',
  'plans.claude.limits.sharedAcrossModels', 'plans.claude.limits.usageCredits', 'plans.claude.limits.subagentsDraw', 'plans.claude.limits.whereToSee',
];
// The sums, worked out by the same code the picture runs, so the reader can redo them.
const opus = priced['claude-opus-5-5']!;
const off = sums(opus, 'off', data.cacheWrite);
const on = sums(opus, 'on', data.cacheWrite);
const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const agents = 20;
const runs = agents * JOBS_PER_HOUR;
const d = (v: number) => `$${+v.toFixed(2)}`;
const pr = { read: d(opus.input), write: d(opus.output), hit: d(opus.cacheRead ?? 0), store: d(opus.input * data.cacheWrite) };
---
<Journey chapter="meter" first="count" data={data} title="What it costs" description="Every turn an agent takes is counted in tokens. On the API each token is on a bill; on a plan they drain an allowance that refills.">
  <Step id="count" title="Every turn is counted">
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p>You just watched twenty agents in <LessonRef id="fleet" />; each works in <Term id="turn">turns</Term>, and on every turn the model reads its whole <Term id="window" /> again. Here is one made-up job, the date fix from <LessonRef id="loop" />, counted in <Term id="token">tokens</Term>.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="technical">
      <p>Each agent in <LessonRef id="fleet" /> works in <Term id="turn">turns</Term>, and every turn sends the whole <Term id="window" /> as input. On the made-up date fix from <LessonRef id="loop" />, the <code>tsc</code> and test output it reads stays in the window and is read again on every later turn.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer">
      <p>Each agent in <LessonRef id="fleet" /> sends its whole <Term id="window" /> as input on every <Term id="turn" />. On the made-up date fix from <LessonRef id="loop" />, input per turn grows with the window, so a job's input grows with the square of its turns.</p>
    </ForLevel>
  </Step>
  <Step id="api" title="On the API, every token is on a bill">
    <p>On the <Term id="api" /> you pay a price per million tokens read and a higher one per million written. Pick a model: the same {JOB_TURNS}-turn date fix runs with the cache off, so the three prices compare like for like.</p>
    <div class="pick">
      {picks.map((id, i) => (
        <button type="button" data-do="model" data-val={id} data-default={i === 0 ? '' : undefined} aria-pressed={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}>{priced[id]!.name}</button>
      ))}
    </div>
    <More title="The prices and the sum">
      <ul>
        {picks.map((id) => (
          <li>{priced[id]!.name}: <Fact id={`models.${id}.price.input`} /> per million read, <Fact id={`models.${id}.price.output`} /> per million written.</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>The bill for {opus.name}, cache off: {n(off.read)} tokens read × {pr.read} + {n(off.written)} written × {pr.write}, per million = {usd3(off.usd)}.</p>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="cache" title="Reading the same thing again costs less">
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p>Claude Code keeps what the model read last turn in a <Term id="cache" />, and on {opus.name} reading it from there costs <Fact id="lineup.cacheReadOpusSonnet" /> the normal price, a twentieth. Turn the cache off, or change the effort level halfway and watch one turn miss it.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is={['technical', 'engineer']}>
      <p>With the <Term id="cache">prompt cache</Term>, the part of the window the last request already sent is billed as a cache read; only the new part is a cache write. Anything that changes the top of the request makes the next turn miss.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="cache" data-val="on" data-default aria-pressed="true">Cache on</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="cache" data-val="off" aria-pressed="false">Cache off</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="cache" data-val="break" aria-pressed="false">Change effort halfway</button>
    </div>
    <More title="The sum, and what breaks the cache">
      <p>On {opus.name} a cache read costs <Fact id="models.claude-opus-5-5.price.cacheRead" /> per million and a cache write <Fact id="lineup.opusCacheWrite5m" /> (<Fact id="lineup.cacheWrite5m" /> the <Fact id="models.claude-opus-5-5.price.input" /> input price). With the cache on: {n(on.cached)} from the cache × {pr.hit} + {n(on.stored)} read new and stored × {pr.store} + {n(on.written)} written × {pr.write}, per million = {usd3(on.usd)}. {Math.round((on.cached / on.read) * 100)}% of the {n(on.read)} tokens read came from the cache.</p>
      <p><Fact id="lineup.cacheBreakers" show="claim" /> <ForLevel is={['technical', 'engineer']} inline><Fact id="lineup.claudeCodeCacheTtl" show="claim" /></ForLevel></p>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="plan" title="On a plan the price is fixed and the amount is not">
    <p>Claude Pro is a <Term id="plan" /> at <Fact id="plans.claude.pro.price" /> a month. Anthropic publishes no figure for how much work its <Term id="allowance" /> buys, so this page cannot say: here it drains at a made-up rate, and refills five hours after your first message.</p>
    <div class="row">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-do="another">Run another date fix</button>
    </div>
    <More title="The limits">
      <p><Fact id="plans.claude.limits.whatUsageMeans" show="claim" /> <Fact id="plans.claude.limits.fiveHour" show="claim" /> <Fact id="plans.claude.limits.weekly" show="claim" /> <Fact id="plans.claude.limits.whereToSee" show="claim" /> <Fact id="plans.claude.limits.usageCredits" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="many" title="Twenty agents draw from one meter">
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p>Back to the <Term id="fleet" />. On a plan the agents share one allowance and stop together when it is empty; on the API they keep going and the bill grows with every agent.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="technical">
      <p>Back to the fleet. <Fact id="plans.claude.limits.subagentsDraw" show="claim" /> On the API every agent adds its own turns to the same bill.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer">
      <p>At twenty agents cost is a design input: cap the turns a job may take, keep model and effort fixed within a thread so the cache keeps hitting, and give routine jobs a model with a lower price.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <div class="controls">
      <label class="control"><span>Agents <output>20</output></span>
        <input type="range" min="1" max="20" step="1" value="20" data-bind="n" />
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="mode" data-val="plan" data-default aria-pressed="true">A plan</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="mode" data-val="api" aria-pressed="false">The API</button>
    </div>
    <More title="The sum for an hour">
      <p>Made-up workload: each agent runs the date fix {JOBS_PER_HOUR} times an hour, with the cache on, at {opus.name}’s real prices. One run is {usd3(on.usd)} (the sum is in the cache step above), so one agent costs {JOBS_PER_HOUR} × {usd3(on.usd)} = {usd3(on.perAgentHour)} an hour, and {agents} agents {usd2(on.perAgentHour * agents)} an hour: {n(on.cached * runs)} × {pr.hit} + {n(on.stored * runs)} × {pr.store} + {n(on.written * runs)} × {pr.write}, per million. Eight hours: {usd2(on.perAgentHour * agents * 8)}.</p>
    </More>
    <Next from="meter" more={[{ href: 'explain/plans-and-api/', text: 'Plans against the API' }, { href: 'models/', text: 'The models and their prices' }]} />
  </Step>
  <Fragment slot="foot">
    <p>The date fix, its token counts, the {JOBS_PER_HOUR} runs an hour and how fast a plan drains are made up. The prices and plan limits are real list figures from the sources below. A coin is five cents on one job and a dollar for the fleet.</p>
    <Verified ids={ids} />
  </Fragment>
</Journey>
